Repairs the ordinary Lighthouse Mission Control navigation failure without changing graph semantics or canonical truth.

Verified production behavior before this patch:
- `canonicalCore.health/currentState/summary/graphNodes/graphEdges/unresolvedRelationships` fan out together during normal Mission Control navigation;
- HTTP calls failed around 8.2–8.5s while underlying PostgreSQL reads continued for ~80–100s after the request died;
- `luminari_action_supporting_bindings_json_v1()` ranked the entire binding universe before filtering one action;
- no `binding_key` is shared across multiple actions in production;
- moving `action_key` before winner ranking reduced a representative helper call from ~282ms to ~1.18ms with identical winner semantics;
- the canonical-state function materialized `SELECT *` from the fully hydrated ~48k-object view despite consuming only six fields;
- an otherwise-identical narrow current-object materialization completed in ~1.47s versus ~6.65s and produced an exact payload match excluding only `generated_at`;
- default graph nodes moved from >8s to ~3.18s with the helper predicate pushdown.

Changes:
1. push the action predicate before binding revision ranking while preserving service-role-only ACL and current-source overlay semantics;
2. narrow `get_lighthouse_canonical_state_v2()` materialization to the six fields its count contract actually uses; all count formulas/live sources remain unchanged;
3. extend the existing PostgreSQL statement-timeout/rollback boundary to `canonical_core_*` read labels without adding queue circuit state;
4. route state/nodes/edges/unresolved reads through that bounded wrapper;
5. share only an in-flight canonical-state promise across simultaneous health/summary/currentState requests; it is cleared immediately on resolve/reject and is not a TTL cache.

No new architecture, no stale snapshot/cache, no graph ownership change, no Docket/Atlas/Rosetta/Prism write changes.

